From: Cargt <tim.carney@cargt.com>
Subject: [PATCH] init-resize: don't mark userfs resized from the initrd

0004 gave this script's userfs case a marker check
(/usr/local/.resize-helper-done, shared with resize-helper.service)
to skip redundant work on subsequent boots - but it also had this
script *write* that marker, unconditionally, right after its own
e2fsck+resize2fs attempt, regardless of whether that attempt actually
grew anything.

On the SD card build, it never does: userfs is a small fixed-size GPT
partition there (200M - see the sdcard .wks.in), and resize2fs only
ever grows a filesystem to fill its *existing* partition boundary - it
can't touch the partition table itself. This script's own resize2fs
call was quietly a no-op that whole time, but wrote the "done" marker
anyway. resize-helper.service - the only script in this BSP with the
tools (sgdisk, resizepart) to actually extend the GPT partition first
- runs later, checks the same marker, sees it already set, and skips
its entire resize block, including the new extend logic added for
exactly this problem. Since this script always runs first (initrd,
before the real rootfs), it permanently wins that race on every single
boot - the marker gets written here before resize-helper.service ever
gets a chance to do the one thing that would actually work.

Confirmed on hardware: added a debug line to resize-helper.service
printing marker state at its own very first line - on a completely
fresh SD card boot, marker was already 1 before resize-helper had done
anything at all. Directly inspecting the built .wic image's userfs
partition region (extracted and read with debugfs) confirmed the
marker is not baked into the image itself - it's written live, this
early, only by this script.

This initrd environment doesn't have sgdisk/resizepart, so it can't
be the one to actually fix userfs on SD card, and it has no way to
tell "grew successfully" apart from "was already at its (still wrong)
size, nothing to do" from resize2fs's exit status alone. Stop writing
the marker here and leave that entirely to resize-helper.service, the
only script that can actually verify (and, on SD card, ensure) it's
true. The fsck+resize2fs attempt itself is left in place unchanged -
on eMMC, where userfs's partition already fills the disk from flash
time, it still does real, useful work early; it's only the marker
write that was wrong.

Upstream-Status: Pending [not sent upstream to ST - specific to a
custom A/B partition scheme]

Signed-off-by: Cargt <tim.carney@cargt.com>
---
diff --git a/init-resize.sh b/init-resize.sh
index 1111111111..2222222222 100644
--- a/init-resize.sh
+++ b/init-resize.sh
@@ -83,11 +83,26 @@ resize_run() {
                                         /sbin/e2fsck -f -y -c -C 0 $DEVICE
                                         /sbin/e2fsck -f -y -C 0 $DEVICE
                                         /sbin/resize2fs $DEVICE
-                                        mkdir -p /tmp/userfs-mark
-                                        mount $DEVICE /tmp/userfs-mark
-                                        touch /tmp/userfs-mark/.resize-helper-done
-                                        umount /tmp/userfs-mark
-                                        rmdir /tmp/userfs-mark
+                                        # Deliberately NOT touching the marker here.
+                                        # On the SD card build, this resize2fs call
+                                        # cannot actually grow userfs - the GPT
+                                        # partition itself is fixed-size here, and
+                                        # only resize-helper.sh (which runs later,
+                                        # in the real rootfs, with sgdisk/resizepart
+                                        # available) can extend that partition table
+                                        # entry before resizing the filesystem into
+                                        # it. This initrd environment doesn't have
+                                        # those tools, and this stage has no way to
+                                        # tell "grew successfully" apart from "already
+                                        # at its (still wrong) size, nothing to do."
+                                        # Marking done here regardless of which one
+                                        # actually happened let this script win the
+                                        # race against resize-helper.sh - it always
+                                        # runs first - permanently skipping the one
+                                        # place that could actually fix userfs on SD
+                                        # card. Leave setting the marker entirely to
+                                        # resize-helper.sh, the only script that can
+                                        # verify (and, on SD card, ensure) it's true.
                                     fi
                                     ;;
                                 root*)
